---
description: Apply at session start on any project — the owner's standard working process (roles, board-as-plan, red-first TDD, three gates, decision logging)
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- Work the way the owner works: the visible process (red→green history, review gates, honest board) is itself the deliverable.
- Phase-separate roles: plan first, implement second, review third — every diff reviewed cold (fresh context) before it lands, with the model tier matched to what's being checked: Haiku-tier for mechanical checks (orphaned imports, dead code after a deletion, deletion completeness, doc drift, test-count deltas, naming and style), Sonnet-tier for ordinary correctness on contained diffs, top tier for adversarial passes on high-stakes paths (data-destructive code, security boundaries, correctness resting on an assumption about an external system) — cold applies at every tier, and the top tier is not for pattern-matching. Where the tool can't route models, keep the phase separation and review your own diff cold instead — the tiering is an optimization, the cold review is not optional. Delegate implementation to cheaper models where the tool supports it. The orchestrator itself runs on the strongest available tier by default; if that tier is not available → offer to switch it to Opus at high reasoning effort instead of silently downgrading.
- The plan lives on a board: tasks as issues (`P<phase>.<seq>`) with scope, a Red-first line (the failing artifact), Done-when criteria, and a DoD checklist. Work discovered mid-task gets an issue first, then a branch — the board never lags reality. No board → `WORKPLAN.md`, same structure.
- One issue = one branch = one PR; nothing to main without a PR; every PR `Closes #N`. Branches `feat/p<N>-<slug>`; conventional commits + `Assisted-by:` trailer.
- Red first, strictly: the failing artifact (unit test / eval case / browser scenario) is committed and visibly failing before implementation. Mock-based tests are paired with real-stack scenarios. Model-inference evals never run in CI — record locally, replay deterministically.
- Three gates on the full PR diff, in order: simplify → security review → code review. Every finding fixed (no follow-up triage), full suite re-run green after fixes, docs PRs included. Fix-first beats ship-with-follow-up. Scale simplify to the diff — a small diff (under ~150 changed lines, or confined to one or two files) gets one combined cheap-tier pass covering all four angles (reuse, simplification, efficiency, altitude), with the deviation stated explicitly; larger or structurally significant diffs keep the full fan-out — structural significance overrides the size heuristic, so a large single-file rewrite is not a small diff. Security review and code review always run at full strength regardless of diff size. Two review→fix rounds on one diff without convergence is the cap — surface to the owner with a recommendation instead of starting a third.
- Log every owner decision the session it's made, in local gitignored `prd/DECISIONS.md` (`Decision / Alternatives rejected / Why`); surface autonomous judgment calls for review. Never publish strategy notes or unverified vulnerability claims.
- Norms: anything that can be done now gets done now; never game a metric (non-deterministic failures stay xfail); when an implementer's measurement against real data contradicts a reviewer's model, the measurement wins — don't spend a review round fixing a finding the implementer already measured not to apply; unattended runs self-merge only after the gates pass, leaving owner-gated items open+annotated. Check in on long-running subagents every 15 minutes — prefer a free spot-check (git status, docker ps, GPU/resource stats, artifact dirs) over spending a message on the agent; confirm real progress from actual output, not just liveness; interrupt and redirect stalled work. CI watches are bounded — a hung run is a failure mode, not a long run: derive the ceiling from the pipeline's known runtime (a suite that finishes in ~30 seconds gets a ~5-minute ceiling, not "until it ends"); never watch unbounded. On breach, diagnose once: a run still queued never started — cancel and re-run it once; a run genuinely executing gets one extension with a stated reason. Two strikes → surface to the owner instead of looping. Right after opening a PR, poll until checks exist (short loop, ~2-minute cap) before reading their absence as a verdict — "no checks reported" immediately post-push is usually the registration race, not the result. Brief subagents with the ceiling/re-run rule, not an open-ended `--watch`; and the 15-minute spot-check of a delegated task includes "is it parked on a CI watch past the ceiling?" (`gh run list` is free). (Observed 2026-07-24: an agent read "no checks reported" pre-registration; an unbounded watch would have slept through a queued-runner stall.) Brief every subagent to never end its turn to "wait" on a background/detached/slow process (no wake-up notification ever arrives) — poll inline in a bounded loop and continue in the same turn.
- First use in a new environment: state once how each element maps to available tooling (gates, board, role split), then follow the mapping — don't silently drop the awkward parts.
